course: dictionary
lesson: 8973
title: Sinner: wrong by the rules, or just naughty
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words change their meaning depending on where you meet them. The context tells you which meaning is right. «Jesus ate with tax collectors and sinners and touched people with leprosy, whom others avoided.» This sentence comes from a religious story, so a sinner is a person who has done something wrong according to a religion's rules.
explain: Now read this one: «I'm a sinner, I ate the last biscuit.» Nobody is talking about religion here. The speaker is joking. Check by swapping in the meaning: «I'm a bit naughty, I ate the last biscuit» still makes sense, so the joking meaning fits.
explain: Two phrases are worth knowing. «Saints and sinners» means all kinds of people, good and bad. «A reformed sinner» means someone who used to do wrong and has since changed for the better. Always ask which meaning fits the words around it.
fact: Sinner means a person who has done something wrong according to a religion's rules.
fact: In a joke, «sinner» means anyone who has done something a bit naughty.
fact: «Sinners» is the plural: it means more than one sinner.
fact: «Saints and sinners» means all sorts of people, good and bad.
fact: «A reformed sinner» means someone who used to do wrong and has changed.
exercise: «Jesus ate with tax collectors and sinners.» In this sentence, what is a sinner? | A person who has lost their way | A person who sells cakes | *A person who has broken a religion's rules | A person who is very tall
exercise: Sam says, «I'm a sinner, I ate the last biscuit.» Is Sam serious about religion? | Yes, Sam has been to prison | *No, Sam is joking that he did something a bit naughty | No, Sam means he is very kind | Yes, Sam is talking about church
exercise: What does «a reformed sinner» mean? | Someone who has never done anything wrong | Someone who is still doing the same wrong thing | *Someone who used to do wrong and has changed for the better | Someone who is very old
test: Which word is the plural of sinner? | *sinners | sinful | sinning | sinned
test: In «saints and sinners», what does the phrase suggest? | Only people who go to church | *People of all kinds, good and bad | Nobody at all | Only people who have done bad things
exam: Which clue tells you what «sinner» means in a sentence? | The picture beside the text | The number of letters in the word | Only the dictionary, never the sentence | *The words around it, such as a religious story or a joking tone
